@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Executing & Modifying Stored Procedures - SQL Server";
}

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold text-primary">
            Executing &amp; Modifying Stored Procedures
        </h1>

        <p class="lead">
            SQL Server Stored Procedures can be executed using the
            <code>EXEC</code> statement and can be modified using
            <code>ALTER PROCEDURE</code>.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         1. INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                1. Introduction
            </h2>

            <p>
                A Stored Procedure is a precompiled collection of SQL
                statements that is stored inside the SQL Server database.
            </p>

            <p>
                After creating a Stored Procedure, we can execute it whenever
                we need its functionality.
            </p>

            <p>
                SQL Server provides the <code>EXEC</code> or
                <code>EXECUTE</code> statement to run a Stored Procedure.
                We can also modify an existing Stored Procedure using
                <code>ALTER PROCEDURE</code>.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         2. EXECUTING A STORED PROCEDURE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white">

            <h3 class="mb-0">
                2. Executing a Stored Procedure
            </h3>

        </div>

        <div class="card-body">

            <p>
                The <code>EXEC</code> statement is used to execute a
                Stored Procedure.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC ProcedureName;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The <code>EXECUTE</code> keyword can also be used.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXECUTE ProcedureName;</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-info mt-3">

                <strong>Note:</strong>

                <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                    <code>EXEC</code> is the commonly used short form of
                    <code>EXECUTE</code>.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         3. SIMPLE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                3. Simple Example
            </h2>

            <p>
                Consider a Stored Procedure that returns all students
                from the <strong>Students</strong> table.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetAllStudents
AS
BEGIN
    SELECT *
    FROM Students;
END;</code></pre>

            <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
                Execute the Procedure
            </h5>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC GetAllStudents;</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">

                <strong>Result:</strong>

                <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                    The Stored Procedure executes the SELECT statement
                    and returns all records from the Students table.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         4. EXECUTING WITH INPUT PARAMETERS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-header bg-primary text-white">

            <h3 class="mb-0">
                4. Executing with Input Parameters
            </h3>

        </div>

        <div class="card-body">

            <p>
                A Stored Procedure can accept input parameters while
                executing.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentById
    @@StudentId INT
AS
BEGIN
    SELECT
        StudentId,
        Name,
        City,
        Age
    FROM Students
    WHERE StudentId = @@StudentId;
END;</code></pre>

            <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
                Execute the Procedure
            </h5>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC GetStudentById
    @@StudentId = 101;</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-info mt-3">

                <strong>Explanation:</strong>

                <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                    Here, <code>101</code> is passed to the
                    <code>StudentId</code> parameter. The procedure
                    returns the student whose StudentId is 101.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         5. EXECUTING WITH MULTIPLE PARAMETERS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                5. Executing with Multiple Parameters
            </h2>

            <p>
                A Stored Procedure can accept multiple input parameters.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentsByCityAndAge
    @@City VARCHAR(50),
    @@Age INT
AS
BEGIN

    SELECT
        StudentId,
        Name,
        City,
        Age
    FROM Students
    WHERE City = @@City
      AND Age = @@Age;

END;</code></pre>

            <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
                Execute
            </h5>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC GetStudentsByCityAndAge
    @@City = 'Pune',
    @@Age = 22;</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">

                <strong>Result:</strong>

                <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                    The procedure returns students whose City is
                    <strong>Pune</strong> and Age is
                    <strong>22</strong>.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         6. EXEC VS EXECUTE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-header bg-info text-dark">

            <h3 class="mb-0">
                6. EXEC vs EXECUTE
            </h3>

        </div>

        <div class="card-body">

            <p>
                SQL Server supports both <code>EXEC</code> and
                <code>EXECUTE</code> for running Stored Procedures.
            </p>

            <div class="row">

                <div class="col-md-6 mb-3">

                    <div class="card border-primary h-100">

                        <div class="card-body">

                            <h5 class="fw-bold text-primary">
                                EXEC
                            </h5>

                            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC GetAllStudents;</code></pre>

                            <p class="mb-0">
                                Short and commonly used form.
                            </p>

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="col-md-6 mb-3">

                    <div class="card border-success h-100">

                        <div class="card-body">

                            <h5 class="fw-bold text-success">
                                EXECUTE
                            </h5>

                            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXECUTE GetAllStudents;</code></pre>

                            <p class="mb-0">
                                Full form of the EXEC statement.
                            </p>

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         7. MODIFYING A STORED PROCEDURE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-header bg-warning">

            <h3 class="mb-0">
                7. Modifying a Stored Procedure
            </h3>

        </div>

        <div class="card-body">

            <p>
                Once a Stored Procedure has been created, its definition
                can be changed using the <code>ALTER PROCEDURE</code>
                statement.
            </p>

            <p>
                We do not need to drop the existing procedure before
                modifying it.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>ALTER PROCEDURE ProcedureName
AS
BEGIN
    -- Modified SQL statements
END;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         8. ALTER PROCEDURE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                8. ALTER PROCEDURE Example
            </h2>

            <p>
                Suppose the following Stored Procedure already exists:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetAllStudents
AS
BEGIN
    SELECT *
    FROM Students;
END;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-4">
                We can modify the procedure to return only selected
                columns.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>ALTER PROCEDURE GetAllStudents
AS
BEGIN

    SELECT
        StudentId,
        Name,
        City
    FROM Students;

END;</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-info mt-3">

                <strong>Explanation:</strong>

                <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                    The existing Stored Procedure is modified using
                    <code>ALTER PROCEDURE</code>. The procedure name
                    remains the same, but its SQL logic is changed.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         9. MODIFYING PARAMETERS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                9. Modifying Procedure Parameters
            </h2>

            <p>
                An existing Stored Procedure can also be modified to
                work with parameters.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>ALTER PROCEDURE GetStudentsByCity
    @@City VARCHAR(50)
AS
BEGIN

    SELECT
        StudentId,
        Name,
        City,
        Age
    FROM Students
    WHERE City = @@City;

END;</code></pre>

            <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
                Execute
            </h5>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC GetStudentsByCity
    @@City = 'Pune';</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         10. MODIFYING PROCEDURE LOGIC
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                10. Modifying Procedure Logic
            </h2>

            <p>
                We can change the SQL statements inside a Stored Procedure
                without changing its name.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>ALTER PROCEDURE GetStudentsByCity
    @@City VARCHAR(50)
AS
BEGIN

    SELECT
        StudentId,
        Name,
        City,
        Age
    FROM Students
    WHERE City = @@City
      AND Age &gt; 18;

END;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                After modification, the procedure returns only students
                whose age is greater than 18.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         11. EXECUTE AFTER MODIFYING
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-header bg-success text-white">

            <h3 class="mb-0">
                11. Execute the Modified Procedure
            </h3>

        </div>

        <div class="card-body">

            <p>
                After modifying the Stored Procedure, we can execute it
                normally using <code>EXEC</code>.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC GetStudentsByCity
    @@City = 'Pune';</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">

                <strong>Result:</strong>

                <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                    SQL Server executes the latest modified definition
                    of the Stored Procedure.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         12. IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">

        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            12. Important Points
        </h4>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li class="mb-2">
                <code>EXEC</code> is used to execute a Stored Procedure.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <code>EXECUTE</code> can also be used to run a procedure.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Input parameters can be supplied while executing a
                Stored Procedure.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Multiple parameters can be passed to a procedure.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <code>ALTER PROCEDURE</code> is used to modify an
                existing Stored Procedure.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                Altering a procedure does not require dropping the
                procedure first.
            </li>

            <li>
                Always test a modified Stored Procedure before using it
                in a production application.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         13. REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white">

            <h3 class="mb-0">
                13. Real-World Example
            </h3>

        </div>

        <div class="card-body">

            <p>
                Consider an application that needs to display students
                from a particular city.
            </p>

            <p>
                First, create the Stored Procedure:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentsByCity
    @@City VARCHAR(50)
AS
BEGIN

    SELECT
        StudentId,
        Name,
        City,
        Age
    FROM Students
    WHERE City = @@City;

END;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-4">
                Execute it:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC GetStudentsByCity
    @@City = 'Pune';</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-4">
                Later, if the application requirement changes and we
                want to return only students above 18 years, modify it:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>ALTER PROCEDURE GetStudentsByCity
    @@City VARCHAR(50)
AS
BEGIN

    SELECT
        StudentId,
        Name,
        City,
        Age
    FROM Students
    WHERE City = @@City
      AND Age &gt; 18;

END;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-4">
                Execute the modified procedure again:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>EXEC GetStudentsByCity
    @@City = 'Pune';</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-secondary mt-3">

                <strong>Use Case:</strong>

                <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                    This approach allows developers to update the business
                    logic of a Stored Procedure without creating a new
                    procedure every time.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         14. SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-primary shadow-sm mb-4">

        <h2 class="fw-bold">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p class="mb-0">
            Stored Procedures are executed using <code>EXEC</code> or
            <code>EXECUTE</code>. Parameters can be supplied during
            execution. Existing Stored Procedures can be modified using
            <code>ALTER PROCEDURE</code> without dropping them first.
            After modification, the procedure can be executed normally
            using <code>EXEC</code>.
        </p>

    </div>


